WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:44.000
This is Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedanta. Each day teachers all over the world try to explain new ideas to their students. Sometimes it goes well. The teacher conveys information, the students absorb it. But many times things get stuck. Students get frustrated and so do their teachers. The transmission of ideas gets bogged down in a morass of failed expectations. I remember an exchange I had with my own father. I must have been around ten. He was trying to teach me a math concept, the order of operations, division before subtraction, multiplication before addition.
这里是Hidden Brain，我是Shankar Vedanta。每天，全世界的老师都在努力向学生解释新概念。有时进展顺利，老师传授知识，学生吸收知识。但很多时候，事情会陷入僵局。学生感到沮丧，老师也一样。知识的传递陷入了期望落空的泥潭。我记得我曾与父亲有过一次交流。那时我大概十岁左右。他试图教我一个数学概念，即运算顺序：先除后减，先乘后加。

2
00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:13.000
I didn't get it. He thought it ought to be easy. He got frustrated, I felt stupid. The worst part was that all the psychological turmoil got us nowhere. At the end of the day, he had failed to teach me something he knew, and I had failed to learn something I could have mastered. In fact, I might have internalized the wrong lessons that I was bad at math or that my father thought poorly of me. What does he think? Why am I so bad? Is he mad?
我没听懂。他觉得这应该很简单。他感到沮丧，我感到自己很笨。最糟糕的是，所有的心理折磨并没有让我们取得任何进展。到最后，他没能教会我他所掌握的知识，而我也没能学会本可以掌握的东西。事实上，我可能内化了错误的教训，认为自己不擅长数学，或者认为父亲看不起我。他在想什么？为什么我这么差劲？他在生气吗？

3
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:57.000
I can't forget that. Disappointing, can I show him? Can I make my dad proud? Today we explore an innovative idea about how we learn. It'll take us from a dolphin exhibit in Hawaii to a top teaching hospital in New York. It's about one method to quiet the noise in our heads, the sort of clutter that can turn learning into a minefield of misery. One evening in the early 1960s, after putting her kids to bed, a young mother sat in her living room and read a small technical manual on dolphin training.
我无法忘记那一刻。令人失望，我能向他证明自己吗？我能让父亲感到骄傲吗？今天，我们将探讨一个关于我们如何学习的创新理念。它将带我们从夏威夷的海豚展览馆走进纽约的一家顶级教学医院。这是一种让大脑安静下来的方法，这种杂乱的思绪会将学习变成一片充满痛苦的雷区。20世纪60年代初的一个晚上，一位年轻的母亲在哄孩子入睡后，坐在客厅里读了一本关于海豚训练的小型技术手册。

4
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:27.000
The ideas in it seemed new and strange. The woman felt an unexpected thrill. As she thumbed through the typewritten pages, she saw for herself a future she had never imagined. It opened a door for me. I saw a whole new game there. How to control behavior, how to build behavior, how to teach skills, all of that. With a little set of clear cut roles that would work with anything. This is Karen Pryor.
书中的观点看起来既新奇又陌生。这位女士感到一种意想不到的兴奋。当她翻阅那些打字机打印出来的页面时，她看到了一个自己从未想象过的未来。它为我打开了一扇门。我在那里看到了一个全新的游戏。如何控制行为，如何建立行为，如何教授技能，所有这些。通过一套清晰明确、适用于任何情况的规则。这就是Karen Pryor。

5
00:02:27.000 --> 00:03:06.000
I'm a biologist and I'm also a writer. Karen is now in her 80s and living in Boston. But back in the early 60s she was in Hawaii, raising three kids, while her husband poured his energy into a grand venture. He was building Sea Life Park, the island's first aquarium and marine entertainment venue. It was his startup, not Carrons. I wasn't involved in the park at all except to maybe give a dinner party for potential investors, you know, that kind of thing. There was a lot at stake. Big dreams and big money had been poured into the project and the timeline for the ground opening was tight.
我是一名生物学家，也是一名作家。Karen现在80多岁了，住在波士顿。但在60年代初，她住在夏威夷，抚养着三个孩子，而她的丈夫则将精力投入到一项宏大的事业中。他正在建造海洋生物公园（Sea Life Park），这是岛上第一个水族馆和海洋娱乐场所。那是他的创业项目，而不是Karen的。我根本没有参与公园的事务，除了可能为潜在投资者举办晚宴之类的活动。当时赌注很大。巨大的梦想和巨额资金投入到了这个项目中，开业的时间表非常紧迫。

6
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:34.000
When the concrete had set in the huge artificial pools, collectors were sent out to capture wild dolphins. The next challenge was to train the animals. The park needed a dolphin trainer, a profession that didn't really exist. The only trainers that were around in that time, in the early 60s, were sea lion trainers from the circus. And they were pretty abusive. Now, the way we've come to think about marine parks
当巨大的水池混凝土凝固后，采集员被派去捕捉野生海豚。接下来的挑战是训练这些动物。公园需要一名海豚训练师，这在当时并不是一个真正的职业。在60年代初，当时唯一的训练师是马戏团的海狮训练师。他们相当粗暴。现在，我们对海洋公园的看法在过去50年里发生了很大变化。让动物表演杂技受到谴责。从野外捕捉鲸鱼和海豚是非法的。但在60年代，限制较少，社会规范也不同。如果说公园及其方法属于那个时代，那么Karen即将学到的课程却是永恒的。公园获得的那本由一位心理学研究生编写的小手册承诺，只要遵循书中描述的技术，任何人都可以成为海豚训练师。

