00:04:48.000 --> 00:05:20.000
work out very well anyway. But he persuaded me to at least read the manual. And that manual, the ideas it held, sparked something inside of Karen. I just had to try it. What Karen saw in the manual was a theory of behavior that it ignited a great debate in psychology in the preceding decades. Its implications were far reaching, especially in the field of education. Let's take a moment to look at that history. In the early 20th century, one dominant view of why people behave the way they do came
弗洛伊德关注的是促使人们做事的深层潜在动机，以及你必须如何深入挖掘那些往往无法触及的人类心理过程来了解一个人。这是心理学家Barry Schwartz。弗洛伊德认为，人们受到非常强烈的动机驱动，其中许多与性有关，许多与父母关系有关，这些动机是如此不可接受，以至于人们将它们排除在意识之外。潜意识，一切都隐藏在大脑的一个小黑盒子里。

10
00:05:20.000 --> 00:06:05.000
from Sigmund Freud. Freud was all about the sort of the deep underlying motivations that got people to do stuff and how you had to plum the depths of your often inaccessible human psychological processes to figure people out. This is psychologist Barry Schwartz. Freud had this view that people were driven by really intense motives, many of them sexual, many of them having to do with relations with their parents, that were so unacceptable that people kept them out of conscious awareness. The unconscious, it was all hidden away in a little black box in the brain.
大约在同一时间，俄罗斯生理学家伊万·巴甫洛夫做出了一个惊人的发现。在一个著名的实验中，他证明了通过正确的触发因素，可以训练动物表现出非自愿的行为。他称之为经典条件反射。巴甫洛夫研究的是他所谓的反射，事实上他称之为心理反射。狗在嘴里有食物时会流口水，这是它们的本能。但如果你先发出一个声音，然后给它们食物，它们就会开始对这个声音产生流口水的反应。这个实验非常出名，甚至还拍摄了纪录片。

11
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Around the same time, the Russian physiologist Ivan Pavlov made a remarkable discovery. In an experiment that became famous, he showed that with the right triggers, an animal could be trained to display involuntary behaviors. He called it classical conditioning. Pavlov was studying what he called reflexes, and in fact he called them psychic reflexes. So dogs salivate when you put food in their mouths and they're built to do that. But if you sound a tone and then give them food, they start salivating to the tone. The experiment has become so famous that documentaries have been made about it.
在把肉通过舱口送进去之前，他引入了一个与喂食完全无关的刺激物。一个滴答作响的节拍器。加入这个组合的是美国心理学家B.F.斯金纳。斯金纳和巴甫洛夫一样，对动物如何学习感兴趣，但他想探索有意识的行为。他最终提出了一种建立在巴甫洛夫理论之上，并完全摒弃了弗洛伊德理论的学说。斯金纳说，你不需要了解潜意识来弄清楚是什么让动物行动，也不需要了解它如何学习。你不需要所有这些深度，你不需要引用“理解内部发生了什么”。

12
00:06:41.000 --> 00:07:19.000
Before passing meat through the hatch, he introduced a stimulus that was totally unrelated to feeding. A ticking metronome. Into this mix, Schema American. B.F. Schinner, like Ivan Pavlov, was interested in how animals learn, but he wanted to explore conscious behavior. He eventually came up with a theory that built on Pavlov and did away entirely with Freud. Skinner said, you don't need to understand the unconscious mind to figure out what makes an animal tick or to get it to learn something. You don't need all this depth, you don't need to, quote, understand what's going on inside,
只需要观察行为和环境如何相互作用就足够了。斯金纳认为，这一切其实都与奖励和惩罚有关，如果你能正确运用它们，你几乎可以让生物做任何你想让它们做的事。对于B.F.斯金纳和其他行为主义者来说，人类行为只关乎你能看到和测量到的东西，而改变行为的关键在于找到正确的激励措施。好的后果随之而来，你就会再次这样做；坏的后果随之而来，你就会停止这样做。B.F.斯金纳的理论变得非常流行。

13
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:55.000
it's enough just to look at how behavior and environment interact. And Skinner had this view that it was really all about rewards and punishments, that if you got them right, you could pretty much get organisms to do whatever you wanted them to do. For BF Skinner and other behaviorists, human behavior was only about what you could see and measure, and changing behavior was all about finding the right incentives. Good consequences follow and you'll do it again, bad consequences follow and you'll stop doing it. B.F. Skinner's theories became wildly popular.
他向挤满的演讲厅和摄制组展示了他的想法。他展示了一个他发明的盒子，并描述了他如何用它来训练鸟类。我们要用饥饿的鸽子，我们要给它们食物。他把一只鸽子放进盒子里。食物由一台小机器提供，它将一个食物托盘升到鸽子够得着的地方。鸽子可以通过空间里的一个开口获得食物。当托盘落下时，鸽子就吃不到了。

14
00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:27.000
He presented his ideas to pack lecture halls and to film crews. He'd display a box he'd invented and describe how he used it with birds. We're going to use hungry pigeons and we're going to give them food. He put a pigeon into his box. And the food is presented with a little machine which lifts a tray of food within reach of the pigeon. Pigeon can get access to it through an opening in the space. And when the tray falls away, that pigeon can no longer eat.
鸽子很快学会了来到机器启动的地方。这是一个简单的例子，但很容易变得更复杂。也许鸽子必须啄一下按钮才能启动机器，或者转个圈。以食物为奖励，它可以学会很多东西。现在我们要尝试另一只鸽子，我会试着挑选出某种特定的行为模式，并使其成为这只鸟行为库中更频繁的一部分。

15
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:55.000
The pigeon quickly learns to come where the machine engages. It's a simple example, but it can easily be made more complex. Maybe the pigeon must pack at a button to engage the machine or turn in a circle. With food as a reward, it can be taught a lot. And we're going to try another pigeon now, and I will try to pick out some particular pattern of behavior and make it a more frequent part of the repertoire of the bird.
行为主义者还有另一个盒子来演示负强化。他把一只老鼠放进盒子里，盒子里有电流会导致它疼痛，但盒子里也有一个可以切断电流的杠杆。老鼠很快学会了直接走到杠杆前，在被放进盒子时关掉电流。B.F.斯金纳的观点是，如果你正确设置了激励和抑制因素，你可以让动物做它们通常不会做的事情。这些想法恰好是Karen Pryor那本小手册的核心。

16
00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:28.000
The behaviorist had another box to demonstrate negative reinforcement. He'd put a rat into the box with an electric current that caused it pain, but the box also had a lever that could switch off the current. The rat quickly learned to go straight to the lever and turn off the electricity when it was placed inside the box. BF Skinner's point was that you can get animals to do things they wouldn't normally do if you set up the incentives and disincentives correctly. These ideas just happened to be front and center in Karen Pryor's little manual.
这是一本关于B.F.斯金纳所称的“操作性条件反射”的入门书。他的意思是学习者是操作者。学习者将有意识地、刻意地做出一种能让自己获益的行为。斯金纳盒子里的鸽子尝试了不同的事情。当它找到托盘时，它得到了食物。实际上，鸽子是在自我教学。

17
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:53.000
It was a primer on what BF Skinner called Operant Conditioning. What he meant by that was that the learner is the operator. The learner is going to deliberately consciously do a behavior that will pay off for the learner. The pigeon in Skinner's box tried different things. When it found the tray, it got the food. The pigeon, in effect, was teaching itself.
它在探索盒子，弄清楚什么有效。Karen对这个想法很着迷，但有一点她不喜欢，那就是手册中内置的所有惩罚措施。比如，如果海豚表现不正确，他们会饿它们24小时。这对这些动物来说是个非常糟糕的主意。它们从食物中获取所有水分，很快就会因脱水而死亡。所以你知道，里面有很多东西我直接跳过了。Karen废除了惩罚，但强调了手册中的另一个想法。

18
00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:26.000
It was exploring the box and figuring out what worked. Karen was enamored with this idea, but one thing she didn't care for was all the punishment built into her manual. Like, if the dolphins didn't behave correctly, they would starve them for 24 hours. That's a very bad idea for these animals. They get all their water from their food and they can die of dehydration pretty quickly. So do you know there were a lot of things in there that I just jumped right away. Karen scrapped the punishments but emphasized another idea from the manual.
她建议将食物奖励与声音信号结合起来。她选择了一个哨子。在某种程度上，这是在巴甫洛夫的基础上建立的。正如巴甫洛夫发现狗会对节拍器的声音流口水一样，手册建议声音可以用来精确标记动物何时做了训练师想要的行为。哨子是一个信号，表示在这一刻你做得完全正确。所以你会得到奖励，但对于学习者来说，识别出你到底做了什么，当你做对时，那个声音信号，那个声学信息，无论它以什么形式出现，那实际上是一种快感。

19
00:10:26.000 --> 00:11:05.000
The suggestion that she combined food rewards with an audible signal. She chose a whistle. In some ways, this was building on Pavlov. Just as Pavlov had found that dogs would salivate at the sound of a metronome, the manual suggested that sounds could be used to precisely mark when an animal did something the trainer wanted. The whistle is the signal that at this very instant you're doing absolutely the right thing. So you're going to get a prize, but the identification for the learner of exactly what you do, what you're when you have got it right, that acoustic sound, that acoustic message, whatever form
